emy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "q-342928" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2{e}^{-3t}+4{e}^{-2t}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "q-342925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=5{e}^{-3t},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "q-342925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{5{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative, $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0),$ and the standard transform \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a,\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-1)+2Y(s) & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)-1 & =\\frac{5}{s+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+2)Y(s) & =1+\\frac{5}{s+3} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+3}{s+3}+\\frac{5}{s+3} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+8}{s+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+8}{(s+2)(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+8}{(s+2)(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{B}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B,$ we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+8=A(s+3)+B(s+2)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2+8=A(-2+3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,6=1A\\,⟹\\,A=6.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n-3+8=A(0)+B(-3+2)\\,⟹\\,5=-1B\\,⟹\\,B=-5.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{6}{s+2}-\\frac{5}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} & ={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =6{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}-5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\ y(t) & =6{e}^{-2t}-5{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "q-342925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "582c2f0bd0ab032eb7c8309378b330eee461f89dd791e2b7db3c90ff8030330c"} {"question" "q-342925" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/