se_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "q-342962" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "q-342960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "q-342960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4-s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n4-s=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n4-(-2)=A((-2)^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,6=20A\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=\\frac{3}{10}+B\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n4=16A+2C\\,⟹\\,4=\\frac{48}{10}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}+\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{10}s-\\frac{2}{5})}{{s}^{2}+16}=\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}-\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{3}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}-\\frac{3}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}}-\\frac{1}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/