ute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c9de028011f1e83dbb0ef31f4ce346d35151d403e6876dfcf3a0e2aba9093fb"} {"question" "e-6657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ for $s > 1$ if \n\n$$\ny' + y =  2e^t, \\qquad y(0)=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/example-6657.json" "file_sha256" "e395389de178bafae976650ad20cc0c519eff529550bb33927a2afb2505b375c"} {"question" "e-6657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{2{e}^{t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =2L{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{ y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'\\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 1\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{ e^t \\}  = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =2L{{e}^{t}} \\\\ (sY(s)+1)+Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{s-1}-1 \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2-(s-1)}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3-s}{{s}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/example-6657.json" "file_sha256" "e395389de178bafae976650ad20cc0c519eff529550bb33927a2afb2505b375c"} {"question" "e-6661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} + 4y = 3e^{-t}, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/1da481c39504266a4f42e13ef938ff0f7eb75d1174a149bed8d80e47e25d46e6.png)\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/ffa301a263d519d3b653c1e1be9b8e8ef59f3cb2fb515e794c00b6b27dadc762.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/54fb8d20b3cd1205ec6ee7c94b74418e45bc239229159c093dbb34bd908f72fd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/example-6661.json" "file_sha256" "cda3303f7a1d129ce75e73bfacdebceb1d51a9645b5cb352b564c21b0f7e43cf"} {"question" "e-6661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} + 4\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = 3\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-t}\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative, $\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0),$ and the standard transform \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\frac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a,\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-2)+4Y(s) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s)-2 & =\\frac{3}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $2$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+4)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{3}{s+1} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2(s+1)}{s+1}+\\frac{3}{s+1} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2s+5}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s + 4)(s+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s+4)(s+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{s+4} + \\dfrac{B}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B,$ we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2s+5 = A(s+1) + B(s+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s = -4,$ we have\n\n$$\n2(-4)+5 = A(-4+1) + B(0) \\implies -3 = -3A \\implies A = 1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s = -1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2(-1)+5 = A(0) + B(-1+4) \\implies 3 = 3B \\implies B = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+4} + \\dfrac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+4}} & ={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} \\\\ y(t) & ={e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/example-6661.json" "file_sha256" "cda3303f7a1d129ce75e73bfacdebceb1d51a9645b5cb352b564c21b0f7e43cf"} {"question" "e-21862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} - 2y = -5\\sin t, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad \\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/476c6ff72b2927d4d86291e5b9816e54f9c7b3fd254b4f8d73de493162a4a4b7.png)\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/8c16e1eed467dbc46fb8698f9a7e2ceff80d47e571506806fe1baf37ec1050ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/a728d0243548e724f95b2331697d75972e05774cf9b9a708c0965361d5e75fc8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/example-21862.json" "file_sha256" "e2671349242dd470e6d0bf7bb00644b615077d705c34c56e507f54cf5232b465"} {"question" "e-21862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} - 2\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = -5\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s^2+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-1)-2Y(s) & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s)-1 & =-\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)Y(s) & =1-\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+1}{{s}^{2}+1}-\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-4}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{s^2 - 4}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{(s-2)(s+2)}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{s+2}{s^2 + 1}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/example-21862.json" "file_sha256" "e2671349242dd470e6d0bf7bb00644b615077d705c34c56e507f54cf5232b465"}] "usage" {"e-21862" [] "e-6657" [] "e-6661" []} "basis" 2642 "retractions" [[17592186147303 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ for $s > 1$ if\n$y' + y = 2e^t, \\qquad y(0)=-1$."] [17592186147303 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′} + y} &= L{2e^{t}} \\\\\nL{y^{′}} + L{y} &= 2L{e^{t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'\\right\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s) + 1\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{e^t \\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′}} + L{y} &= 2L{e^{t}} \\\\\n(sY(s) + 1) + Y(s) &= \\frac{2}{s - 1} \\\\\n(s + 1)Y(s) &= \\frac{2}{s - 1} - 1 \\\\\n(s + 1)Y(s) &= (2 - (s - 1))/(s - 1) \\\\\nY(s) &= \\frac{3 - s}{s^{2} - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147304 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} + 4y = 3e^{-t}, \\qquad y(0)=2$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}$. Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y$, we have\n\n$AAAAAAAA_{A}^{A}$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$AAAAAAAA_{A}^{A}$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$AAAAAAAA_{A}^{A}$"] [17592186147304 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} + 4\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = 3\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-t}\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0)\n$$\n\nand the standard transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\frac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(sY(s) - 2) + 4Y(s) &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 1} \\\\\n(s + 4)Y(s) - 2 &= \\frac{3}{s + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s)$. First, add $2$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s + 4)Y(s) &= 2 + \\frac{3}{s + 1} \\\\\n(s + 4)Y(s) &= (2(s + 1))/(s + 1) + \\frac{3}{s + 1} \\\\\n(s + 4)Y(s) &= \\frac{2s + 5}{s + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s + 4)(s+1)}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s+4)(s+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{s+4} + \\dfrac{B}{s+1}\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B$, we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2s+5 = A(s+1) + B(s+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B$.\n\n- Setting $s = -4$, we have\n$2(-4)+5 = A(-4+1) + B(0) \\implies -3 = -3A \\implies A = 1$.\n- Setting $s = -1$, we have\n$2(-1)+5 = A(0) + B(-1+4) \\implies 3 = 3B \\implies B = 1$.\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+4} + \\dfrac{1}{s+1}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 4}} &= e^{-4t} \\\\\nL^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 1}} &= e^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 4}} + L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 1}} \\\\\ny(t) &= e^{-4t} + e^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147172 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} - 2y = -5\\sin t, \\qquad y(0)=1$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad \\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0$.\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}$. Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y$, we have\n\n$AAAAA_{A}^{A}AA$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$AAAAA_{A}^{A}AA$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$AAAAA_{A}^{A}AA$"] [17592186147172 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} - 2\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = -5\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0)\n$$\n\nand the standard trigonometric transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s^2+1}\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(sY(s) - 1) - 2Y(s) &= -5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s^{2} + 1} \\\\\n(s - 2)Y(s) - 1 &= -\\frac{5}{s^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s)$. First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s - 2)Y(s) &= 1 - \\frac{5}{s^{2} + 1} \\\\\n(s - 2)Y(s) &= \\frac{s^{2} + 1}{s^{2} + 1} - \\frac{5}{s^{2} + 1} \\\\\n(s - 2)Y(s) &= \\frac{s^{2} - 4}{s^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s-2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{s^2 - 4}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{(s-2)(s+2)}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{s+2}{s^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{s}{s^{2} + 1}} &= \\cos t \\\\\nL^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s^{2} + 1}} &= \\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= L^{-1}{\\frac{s}{s^{2} + 1}} + 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s^{2} + 1}} \\\\\ny(t) &= \\cos t + 2\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
